import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../Ui/Versus

## File-based routing, as SvelteKit has it: this site's own pages are routed this way.
meta = {
	title: "Routing",
	description: "File routing in Roc Solid: pages, layouts, the error page and endpoints are files under routes/, params are directory names, a directory's middleware guards its routes, links navigate without a reload, and every page gets a typed path, every endpoint a typed function.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "files", label: "The files" },
		{ id: "page", label: "A page" },
		{ id: "layouts", label: "Layouts" },
		{ id: "params", label: "Params, and typed links" },
		{ id: "error", label: "Where no page is" },
		{ id: "endpoints", label: "Endpoints: +server.roc" },
		{ id: "middleware", label: "Middleware: what runs, in what order" },
		{ id: "navigating", label: "Navigating without a reload" },
		{ id: "written", label: "What rocx writes" },
		{ id: "router", label: "The router" },
		{ id: "openapi", label: "The OpenAPI document" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Routing"
				title="A page is a file"
			>
				An app's URLs come from the files under its <code>routes/</code> directory, as in SvelteKit. A
				<code>+page.rocx</code> is the page at its directory's path, wrapped by the
				<code>+layout.rocx</code> files above it. A <code>+server.roc</code> answers HTTP requests at its
				path, and <code>+error.rocx</code> is shown where nothing answers. Every page gets a typed path and
				every endpoint a typed function, so a link to a missing page, or a call to a missing endpoint, fails
				to compile.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="files">The files</Heading>
			<p>This site's route files, one for every page you can open here:</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_tree.file} source={Sources.route_tree.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain />
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>routes/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/blog</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/blog/anything</code>, given <code>{"params.slug"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>+layout.rocx</code></td><td>wraps every page in its directory and below it</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/+error.rocx</code></td><td>what a browser sees at a path nothing answers (404), and when a page fails</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/+server.roc</code></td><td>the endpoints at <code>/blog</code>: <code>get!</code>, <code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>, <code>patch!</code> and <code>delete!</code>; <code>events!</code>, a stream of events; <code>channel!</code>, a channel pages subscribe to; <code>respond!</code>, every other method</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/admin/+middleware.roc</code></td><td>the middleware around every page, endpoint and channel in <code>admin/</code> and below it (<a href="#middleware">below</a>); <code>routes/+middleware.roc</code> runs around every request</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/(members)/…</code></td><td>a group: a directory in brackets adds nothing to the path, so files at different paths share its middleware and layout</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/.well-known/…</code></td><td>routed, as RFC 8615 asks; any other directory whose name starts with a dot is skipped</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Each directory names one part of the path, in lower case (<code>blog</code>,
				<code>getting-started</code>, <code>feed.xml</code>), or a param in brackets (<code>[slug]</code>).
				It may start with an underscore, for a path another service names (<code>_verify</code> is
				<code>verify</code> in <code>Api</code> and <code>Paths</code>). <code>rocx build</code> refuses
				other names, and says why.
			</p>

			<Heading id="page">A page</Heading>
			<p>
				A route file has no module header: imports, then definitions. A page defines <code>view!</code>,
				the component that renders it, and may define <code>meta</code>, a record its layouts are given. On
				this site, <code>meta</code> holds the title, the description and the table of contents on the
				right:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_page.file} source={Sources.route_page.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>view!</code> is given the page's params:</strong> <code>{"view! = |{ params }| …"}</code>,
					a record of strings, or of the <a href="#params">types its handlers give them</a>
					(<code>{"{}"}</code> without params; write <code>|_|</code>). A page is an
					<a href={"${Paths.components}#kinds"}>effectful component</a>: its body runs once, and it may make
					signals.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Its data comes from endpoints.</strong> A page isn't given the request. It reads its
					location with <code>{"Router.location!({})"}</code> and its data with <code>Endpoint.read!</code>,
					answered by the server as it renders (<a href={Paths.calls}>talking to the server</a>). What
					middleware knows, such as who is signed in, comes the same way, from an endpoint behind it
					(<a href="#middleware">below</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Imports are relative to the file.</strong> This one is two directories down, so the site's
					components are under <code>../../Ui</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A route file can define components of its own</strong>
					(<code>{"Board := [].{ view! = … }"}</code>) and use them as <code>{"<Board />"}</code>. Put them
					before or after the file's definitions, not between: rocx writes them as types beside the page's
					module, so their <code>view!</code> isn't taken for the page's
					(<a href={"${Paths.components}#title"}>an example</a>). They can use the file's other definitions;
					a name a component defines itself is its own.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="layouts">Layouts</Heading>
			<p>
				A <code>+layout.rocx</code> wraps every page in its directory and below it. Layouts nest, the
				outermost first. The root one, <code>routes/+layout.rocx</code>, renders the whole document
				(<code>{"<html>"}</code>, <code>{"<head>"}</code>, <code>{"<body>"}</code>); without one, an app
				gets a plain document. A layout's <code>view!</code> is given:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong><code>children</code></strong>: the page, or the next layout in;</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>params</code></strong>: the ones its directory names
					(<code>routes/[lang]/+layout.rocx</code> gets <code>lang</code>; the root layout gets
					<code>{"{}"}</code>);
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>meta</code></strong>: the page's <code>meta</code>. Every page under a layout that
					takes it must give it the same type; <code>rocx build</code> reports a page whose
					<code>meta</code> has other fields, or none. Where no page is, the error page's <code>meta</code>
					stands in.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				On navigation, a layout stays, with its state, while its file and its own params are the same
				(<a href="#navigating">navigating</a>). This site's one layout, the document around every page,
				reads the location where it uses it, so it is kept:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_layout.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_layout.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="params">Params, and typed links</Heading>
			<p>
				A directory in brackets is a param: <code>routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx</code> is the page at any
				<code>/blog/…</code>, and its <code>view!</code> is given <code>{"params : { slug : Str }"}</code>.
				A param is a string, decoded from the URL, unless a handler's annotation gives it another type.
				Fixed parts are tried before params, so <code>/blog/new</code> can be a page of its own beside
				<code>/blog/[slug]</code>. A directory may name a file, with dots between letters or digits:
				<code>routes/sitemap.xml/+server.roc</code> answers <code>/sitemap.xml</code>, and its names drop
				the dot (<code>Api.get_sitemap_xml</code>). A page from the app the tests build:
			</p>
			<Code title="test/e2e/routes/routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx" source={Sources.route_fixture_page.rocx} />
			<p>
				rocx also writes <code>Paths</code>: a string for each page without params, and for each page with
				them, a function that encodes them into the URL. Link through it, and a link to a missing page, or
				with the wrong params, fails to compile at your line
				(<code>{"Paths.blog_slug({ slg: \"x\" })"}</code>: <em>Maybe slg should be slug?</em>).
				<code>Paths.all</code> lists the pages without params.
			</p>
			<Code title={"What rocx writes for that app: ${Sources.route_fixture_paths.file}"} source={Sources.route_fixture_paths.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<strong>Typed params.</strong> A handler's annotation may give a param a type other than
				<code>Str</code>: a number, <code>Bool</code>, or a union of tags written out
				(<code>{"[Draft, Live]"}</code>). The router reads the path's part as that type first; a part
				that doesn't read is no match, so <code>/api/count/five</code> is a 404 the handler never sees. The
				type is the directory's: its handlers must agree (<code>rocx build</code> says where two differ),
				and its pages, layouts, <code>Paths</code> and <code>Api</code> take it too. A page whose endpoints
				are under <code>api/</code> declares its own, <code>{"Params : { id : I64 }"}</code>: rocx never
				reads a page's types from another directory. This site's count of
				<a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>events</a> is typed:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_count.file} source={Sources.endpoint_count.rocx} />
			<p>This site's sidebar is built from <code>Paths</code>, each part's pages grouped under headings:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_nav.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_nav.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="error">Where no page is</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>routes/+error.rocx</code> is the app's error page. A browser asking for a path nothing
				answers gets it, inside the root layout, with a 404; other clients get a plain-text 404. A page that
				fails shows it too, unless an <a href={"${Paths.components}#waiting"}><code>{"<Errored>"}</code></a>
				inside catches the failure. Its status is the 4xx an endpoint refused a read with
				(<code>NotFound</code> makes the page a 404), or else 500. The error page's <code>view!</code> is
				given <code>status</code> and <code>problem</code>, why it failed, as text.
				Without one, an app gets a plain one. This site's lists the pages of the docs' part the path is in
				(try <a href="/nowhere">/nowhere</a> or <a href="/platform/nowhere">/platform/nowhere</a>):
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_error.file} source={Sources.route_error.rocx} />

			<Heading id="endpoints">Endpoints: +server.roc</Heading>
			<p>
				A <code>+server.roc</code> (<code>+server.rocx</code> if it has markup) answers HTTP requests at its
				directory's path, with a handler per method: <code>get!</code>, <code>post!</code>,
				<code>put!</code>, <code>patch!</code> or <code>delete!</code>. A handler gets the request, the path's
				params, its middleware's context and, for a method with a body, the body as its annotated type. It
				answers <code>Ok(result)</code>, sent as JSON with a 200, or <code>Err(refusal)</code>, such as
				<code>BadRequest(why)</code> (400) or <code>NotFound</code> (404). Annotated
				<code>{"Try(Server.Response, Endpoint.Refusal)"}</code>, it answers with its own response: a redirect,
				a cookie, a file that isn't JSON. This one answers the form on this site's
				<a href={Paths.members_join}>joining page</a> and sets a cookie:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.members_join_server.file} source={Sources.members_join_server.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Bodies.</strong> JSON (<code>application/json</code>) and forms (URL-encoded, or
					<code>multipart/form-data</code> with files) are read; a missing or unreadable field is a 400 that
					names it. Any other body, such as raw bytes, isn't: the handler takes <code>{"{}"}</code> as its body
					and reads the bytes from <code>request.body</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Checked bodies.</strong> Beside the handler, <code>post_body = Signup.form</code> (a form of
					<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_forms}#endpoint"}>packages/forms</a>) reads and checks the body, form or
					JSON, before the handler runs; the handler's <code>body</code> is the rules' record. A body that
					fails is a <code>422</code> with the issues as JSON, and <code>/openapi.json</code> documents it with
					the rules' schema (D-093).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Three more names.</strong> <code>events!</code> answers a GET that asks for events
					(<code>accept: text/event-stream</code>) with a stream (<a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>events</a>).
					<code>channel!</code> defines a channel pages subscribe to
					(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#channels"}>channels</a>). <code>respond!</code>, given the request, params
					and context, answers every method the file doesn't name with its own <code>Server.Response</code>: a
					page mounted from elsewhere, a JSON-RPC endpoint.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A path is a page or an endpoint.</strong> A page's endpoints (what it reads and what its
					forms post to) live under <code>routes/api/</code>, at the page's path: the page at
					<code>/members</code> reads <code>GET /api/members</code>. One URL, one answer, so no cache mixes them
					up. A directory may still hold both: a browser's GET (accepting HTML) gets the page, anything else
					the endpoints, with <code>Vary: Accept</code>. An unanswered method is a 405 listing the answered
					ones (<code>Allow</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Typed at both ends.</strong> Each handler must be annotated: rocx copies the annotations
					into <code>Generated/Api.roc</code>, which any module may import (a component, a job, an actor), and
					binds each handler in <code>Generated/Handlers.roc</code>, on the server only
					(<a href="#written">what rocx writes</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A form posted without JavaScript</strong> goes back to its page with a flash the page reads
					(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#forms"}>forms</a>): JSON would show as a bare page.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<a href={"${Paths.calls}#endpoints"}>Talking to the server</a> shows the rest, with this site's
				endpoints live.
			</p>

			<Heading id="middleware">Middleware: what runs, in what order</Heading>
			<p>
				Middleware runs around the answer to a request. Given the request, the context so far and
				<code>next!</code> (what comes after it), it can answer itself (a guard's redirect or 401), pass the
				request on with more context (who is signed in), or change the answer coming back (add a header).
				Guards, sessions, security headers and logs are middleware; <code>a.then(b)</code> runs
				<code>a</code>, then <code>b</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.middleware_type.file}, in part"} source={Sources.middleware_type.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Middleware goes by folder. A <code>+middleware.roc</code> names the middleware around every page,
				endpoint and channel in its directory and below; nothing else runs any. The one in
				<code>routes/</code> is the app's, around every request (a package's handlers and paths nothing has
				included). This site's adds two headers to every answer:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.site_root_middleware.file} source={Sources.site_root_middleware.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Below it, each runs inside the ones above, outermost first; what one hands on is the
				<code>context</code> of the next and of the handlers inside
				(<code>{"get! = |{ context, .. }| …"}</code>). The members' page and its endpoint,
				<code>/members</code> and <code>/api/members</code>, sit in a group, <code>routes/(members)/</code>,
				and share one guard; the joining page, <code>routes/members/join/</code>, has its own. Before any of
				them, a change sent by another site's page (POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE) is refused, unless
				<code>App.program</code> says <code>cross_site: Allowed</code>. This site's middleware:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.site_middlewares.file} source={Sources.site_middlewares.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.site_members_middleware.file} source={Sources.site_members_middleware.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Versus>
				<Code title={"${Sources.members_page.file}, in part"} source={Sources.members_page.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title={Sources.members_server.file} source={Sources.members_server.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			</Versus>
			<p>
				A request to <code>/members</code> goes through <code>routes/+middleware.roc</code>'s middleware,
				then the group's <code>Middlewares.member</code>, then the page, and the answer goes back out through
				them. The page isn't given the context: it reads <code>GET /api/members</code>, which the server
				answers through the endpoint's route, behind the same middleware, so the handler gets the member's
				name. A handler's <code>Endpoint.send!</code> and an MCP tool made from an endpoint go that way too:
				what guards an endpoint guards every way to it. <code>rocx routes</code> lists every route and what
				runs around it, outermost first:
			</p>
			<Code title="A terminal" source={Sources.route_list.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The context is typed.</strong> <code>member</code> is a
					<code>{"Middleware({ facts : Str }, { facts : Str, member : Str })"}</code>: it adds
					<code>member</code> to the context it is given. The members' endpoint can read
					<code>context.member</code>; outside the group, that wouldn't compile.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A new file is guarded by where it is.</strong> A page or endpoint added to the group runs
					behind its middleware with no line of its own. A route file can't name middleware:
					<code>rocx build</code> refuses <code>middleware = …</code> in a page, an endpoint, a layout or the
					error page, and says which <code>+middleware.roc</code> to move it to.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Only some requests.</strong> <code>{"Middleware.when(Server.changes, guard)"}</code> runs
					<code>guard</code> only for requests that change something, and lets reads by: a rate limit on a
					form's posts, say.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Try it with <code>just dev</code>.</strong> <a href={Paths.members}>/members</a> sends you to
					join; join, and it greets you by name. These pages aren't in the static copy of this site, which
					has no server.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="navigating">Navigating without a reload</Heading>
			<p>
				A click on a link to another of the app's pages doesn't reload, as with Solid Router. The runtime
				fetches the new page's HTML document as a reload would: from the server, through its middleware,
				with reads answered and redirects followed (on a static copy, the file the build wrote). The app's
				wasm already holds every page's code, so it renders the new page from the document's answers and
				<code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> parts, and asks for nothing else. Every link works this way, with no
				change to the app.
			</p>
			<p>
				The old page stays until the document is in. Then the address is pushed to the history, the new
				page is made, and its <code>{"<head>"}</code> is updated (loaded stylesheets stay). The page scrolls
				to the top or to the link's <code>#hash</code>, focus goes where a reload would put it (an
				<code>autofocus</code> element, or the page's start), and a screen reader hears the new title. Back
				and forward render their entry's page the same way and restore its scroll. A document fetched in the
				last ten seconds is reused.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Write</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>{"<a href={Paths.blog_slug({ slug })}>"}</code></td><td>renders the page in place. Any plain link does: in markup, in Markdown, inside a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<a href={…} replace noscroll>"}</code></td><td>replaces the history entry instead of pushing one; keeps the scroll where it is</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"rel=\"external\""}</code>, <code>target</code>, <code>download</code>, a modifier key</td><td>left to the browser: a reload, a new tab, a download</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"navigate!(Paths.blog_slug({ slug }))"}</code></td><td>in a handler: goes to that page once the handler is done. <code>{"navigate_with!(to, { replace, scroll })"}</code> takes Solid Router's options (<code>{"import pf.Router exposing [navigate!, navigate_with!]"}</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Router.is_routing!({})"}</code></td><td>whether a navigation is on its way, tracked: a progress bar's <code>{"class:on={…}"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Router.location!({})"}</code></td><td>where the page is, <code>{"{ path, query }"}</code>, tracked: a hole that reads it follows each navigation</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"a[aria-current=\"page\"]"}</code>, <code>{"a[aria-current=\"true\"]"}</code></td><td>every link to one of the app's pages says where the page is: <code>page</code> at its path (and its query, if it has one), <code>true</code> at a path under it (never <code>/</code>), kept right as you navigate. A link that writes its own <code>aria-current</code> is left alone. Style with CSS</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"search = use_search_params!({})"}</code></td><td>the query: <code>{"search.get!(\"days\")"}</code> and <code>{"search.all!()"}</code> read it, tracked; <code>{"search.set!([(\"days\", \"30\")])"}</code>, in a handler, merges into it (<code>{"\"\""}</code> takes a name out) and changes it in place (<code>{"import pf.Router exposing [use_search_params!]"}</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<Navigate href={Paths.sign_in} />"}</code></td><td>sends the visitor elsewhere as the page renders: a 303 from the server, or, in the browser, a navigation that replaces the history entry (<code>{"import pf.Navigate"}</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"use_before_leave!(|leave| …)"}</code></td><td>in a component: asked before each navigation away; it may stop it (<code>{"leave.prevent!()"}</code>) and let it go later (<code>{"leave.retry!(Bool.True)"}</code>). Solid Router's <code>useBeforeLeave</code> (<code>{"import pf.Router exposing [use_before_leave!]"}</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<form action=\"/search\">"}</code></td><td>a GET form: navigates in place to its URL, with its fields as the query</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<html data-rocx-navigation=\"off\">"}</code></td><td>every link reloads the page instead; <code>"explicit"</code> navigates in place only for a link marked <code>data-rocx-link</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"data-rocx-reload"}</code></td><td>on a link, a form or an element around them: left to the browser, a load or a plain post (SvelteKit's <code>data-sveltekit-reload</code>); <code>{"\"off\""}</code> takes it back for what is inside</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"data-rocx-preload=\"off\""}</code>, <code>{"\"viewport\""}</code></td><td>on a link or an element around it (<code>{"<html>"}</code> for the whole app): never prefetch; or prefetch as the link comes into sight, as well as on hover, focus and touch</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<html data-rocx-transitions>"}</code></td><td>makes each navigation a View Transition, the browser's cross-fade, where the browser has them and the visitor hasn't asked for less motion. This site's bar and sidebar have a <code>view-transition-name</code>, so they stay put while the page changes</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Layouts are kept.</strong> Each layout below the root one, and the page, is a level, made
					anew only when its file or its own params change: from one blog post to the next, the blog's layout
					keeps its sidebar's scroll, an open menu and its signals. A level that reads its location once, at
					the top of its body (<code>{"path = Router.location!({}).path"}</code>), would show a stale location,
					so the runtime remakes it on every navigation and a dev build warns in the console. Read the
					location where you use it, in markup or a memo. A kept level's <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> parts
					come from the new document.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A change of the query alone happens in place.</strong> A link to <code>?days=30</code>,
					<code>{"search.set!"}</code> or <code>{"navigate!(\"?days=30\")"}</code> fetches no document: the
					page and layouts stay, and what read the query reads it again. A read whose URL changed asks its
					endpoint (behind its middleware), showing its last value meanwhile. Back goes to the previous query.
					A <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> doesn't follow the query: the browser has no code for it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The server decides, as on a reload.</strong> The document is a page's GET with
					<code>x-rocx-navigate: 1</code>, so middleware that counts views can tell it from a reload. Guards
					refuse or redirect as usual, and a redirect lands at its address. The browser reloads instead when
					the target isn't one of the app's pages (a plugin's page, a file, a path no page has), when the
					answer isn't a document of this build (a text 401, a crash, a new deploy's page), or when the fetch
					fails.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A link is fetched before it is clicked.</strong> Hovering, focusing or touching a link
					prefetches its document (<code>x-rocx-navigate: preload</code>), so the click asks nothing. A
					prefetch renders a page that may never be seen: middleware that counts views should skip those, and
					an app with costly pages can turn prefetching off.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Leaving unsaved work can ask first.</strong> A component holding unsaved work registers a
					listener with <code>{"use_before_leave!"}</code>. Before each navigation away (a link,
					<code>{"navigate!"}</code>, a form's redirect, a query change, back and forward), it is told where to
					(<code>to</code>, <code>from</code>, and for back and forward how far, <code>delta</code>). It may
					stop the navigation, keeping the page and its address, or hold it while the app asks;
					<code>{"leave.retry!(Bool.True)"}</code> then lets it go, skipping the listeners
					(<code>{"Bool.False"}</code> asks them again). A listener decides from what the page has, without
					asking the server, and goes with its component. A real page load (another site, a reload, the
					tab closed) can't be held: when a listener would stop one, the browser asks the visitor itself. The
					<a href={Paths.examples_tasks}>tasks example</a>'s task page does this.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Page scripts run on a load only.</strong> A navigation doesn't run the new page's
					<code>{"<script>"}</code>s. Script that sets up on <code>rocx:ready</code> should also do so on
					<code>rocx:navigated</code>, a document event with the URL and how long the navigation took (this
					site's inspectors do).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What the old page started stops with it.</strong> Its effects' cleanups run, its timers and
					event streams stop, and its channels are left, unless the next page subscribes to one too.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="written">What rocx writes</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> turns the routes into Roc modules under <code>Generated/</code>. You never
				edit them, but knowing them helps you read the compiler's reports:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>ShowcasePage.roc</code>, <code>ApiMembersServer.roc</code>…</td><td>each route file, as a module named after its path</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Paths.roc</code></td><td>a path for every page (<a href="#params">typed links</a>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Api.roc</code></td><td>the app's contract: a function for each endpoint and channel, with its method, URL and types</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Handlers.roc</code></td><td>the server's only: each endpoint bound to its handler</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Routes.roc</code></td><td>the server's router: the answer to a request (<a href="#router">below</a>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Root.roc</code></td><td>the pages as one component, in their layouts: what the server renders and the browser hydrates and navigates</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>App.roc</code></td><td>the server's program, which <code>main.roc</code> finishes with <code>App.program</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Styles.roc</code></td><td>the app's stylesheet, from the components' scoped <code>{"<style>"}</code>s</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Roc names a module after its file, and <code>+page</code> isn't a valid name, so each route file
				becomes a module named after its path (<code>routes/showcase/+page.rocx</code> is
				<code>ShowcasePage</code>). rocx keeps the lines where they were: the module's first line goes on
				the blank line after the imports (or, with none, at the start of the first definition's line). Line
				<var>N</var> of the module is line <var>N</var> of the route file, so the compiler's reports point
				at your lines:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_page_generated.file} source={Sources.route_page_generated.rocx} />

			<Heading id="router">The router</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Generated/Routes.roc</code>'s <code>respond!</code> answers a request. It splits the path into
				parts and tries each route, fixed paths before params. A match answers with the page inside its
				layouts (for a browser's GET), the endpoint for the method, or a 405; no match, with the error page:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_routes.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_routes.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>App.program</code> answers every request with it, behind
				<code>routes/+middleware.roc</code>'s middleware: every URL is a route file (D-116), the OpenAPI
				document too. A route under a <code>+middleware.roc</code> is wrapped in
				<code>Middleware.run!</code>, one inside the other, outermost first, and the handler gets what the
				innermost passed on. The members page and its endpoint run behind their group's:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_members.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_members.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="openapi">The OpenAPI document</Heading>
			<p>
				An app can serve an OpenAPI document (3.2) of its endpoints, for client generators or people
				exploring the API. It lists:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>each path and its params (a <a href="#params">typed param</a> with its type's schema);</li>
				<li>
					each method, with the doc comment above its handler. The comment's first sentence, without a
					method and path it may start with, is the summary Swagger UI and Scalar list the method by:
					<code>## POST /api/shout: what it is sent, shouted.</code> gives <em>What it is sent, shouted.</em>;
				</li>
				<li>
					the JSON Schemas of what each endpoint is sent and answers: the handler's types as the compiler
					has them. This site's <code>{"GET /api/shout/{word}"}</code> answers
					<code>{"{ word : Str, shouted : Str, bytes : U64 }"}</code>, and its document says so.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Pages' endpoints are in it too. A body a form can send (a record of text, numbers, yes-or-no,
				choices and lists of them) is listed three ways: <code>application/json</code>,
				<code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> and <code>multipart/form-data</code>, with one schema,
				except that a form may leave out a yes-or-no or a list, as it leaves out an unticked box. A body
				checked by rules (<a href="#endpoints"><code>post_body</code></a>) is listed the same three ways,
				with the rules' schema: what they require, and their lengths, patterns and ranges.
			</p>
			<h3>Turning it on</h3>
			<p>
				An app serves it from routes of its own (D-116): <code>routes/openapi.json/+server.roc</code>
				answers with the document, and <code>routes/openapi.html/+server.roc</code> with a page that shows
				it in Swagger UI or Scalar. Without them, neither is served.
			</p>
			<Code title="routes/openapi.json/+server.roc" source={"import pf.OpenApi\nimport ../../Generated/Api\n\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| OpenApi.document_route(request, Api.openapi)"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title="routes/openapi.html/+server.roc" source={"import pf.OpenApi\n\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| OpenApi.page_route(request, Swagger)"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>They are routes like any other</strong>: their folder's middleware runs around them (put
					them in a guarded group to keep the document from strangers), and <code>rocx routes</code> lists
					them. Under <code>--dev</code>, the server lists the page as it starts.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The page loads its viewer from jsDelivr</strong>, pinned to one version and checked
					against its hash, and tells it not to call home: no validator for Swagger UI; no telemetry, fonts,
					developer tools or agent for Scalar. Bundling a viewer instead would add 2 to 5 MB to every app's
					server.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				This site serves both: <a href="/openapi.html">its API, in Swagger UI</a>, and
				<a href="/openapi.json">the document</a>.
			</p>
			<h3>Who makes it, and when</h3>
			<p>
				Neither platform makes it: rocx writes Roc that asks for it, the compiler works it out, and the
				server only sends it.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What runs</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rocx build</code></td>
							<td>rocx (<code>rocx-cli/Routes.roc</code>), as it writes the router</td>
							<td>
								Writes Roc, knowing no types. <code>Generated/Api.roc</code> gets <code>openapi</code>: each
								path with its params, methods and comments, and for each endpoint a call that asks
								<code>OpenApi</code> for the schemas of its body and its answer. It also gets
								<code>tags</code>, every name in the app's code that could be a tag.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>roc build</code>, the server's</td>
							<td>the Roc compiler</td>
							<td>
								Infers each endpoint's types and writes, for each, a parser for the format
								<code>OpenApi.Schema</code>, as it writes one for <code>Json</code> to read the endpoint's
								body. <code>openapi</code> is a constant, so the compiler works out its value as it compiles:
								the server's binary holds the document as text.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>GET /openapi.json</code></td>
							<td>the server (native)</td>
							<td>Sends that text, if the app turned it on, behind its middleware, like every answer.</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The browser's build (wasm) has none of it: its <code>Api</code> is the same contract (each
				endpoint's method, URL and types) without the document. What rocx writes for this site, in part:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.openapi_api.file}, in part"} source={Sources.openapi_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<h3>A parser that writes</h3>
			<p>
				Roc has no reflection: no code can ask what a type is. But the parser the compiler writes for a type
				works with any format: it calls the format shape by shape (a record, its next field, a string, a
				number), handing a record's format its field names. <code>OpenApi.Schema</code> is a format that
				reads nothing: for each part, it writes that part's schema and gives back a value nothing looks at.
				<code>OpenApi.body</code> asks the compiler for the parser of what the endpoint is sent, for
				<code>Schema</code>, and runs it:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.openapi_format.file}, in part"} source={Sources.openapi_format.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				For the answer of <code>{"GET /api/shout/{word}"}</code>,
				<code>{"{ word : Str, shouted : Str, bytes : U64 }"}</code>, the parser and <code>Schema</code> go:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The parser calls</th><th><code>Schema</code> writes</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_start</code></td><td><code>{"{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code>, given the names <code>bytes</code>, <code>shouted</code>, <code>word</code>: it hands back <code>bytes</code></td><td><code>{"\"bytes\":"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_u64</code>, as <code>bytes</code> is a <code>U64</code></td><td><code>{"{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0}"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code> and <code>parse_str</code>, for <code>shouted</code>, then <code>word</code></td><td><code>{",\"shouted\":{\"type\":\"string\"}"}</code>, and <code>word</code>'s</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code>, with no name left: it says <code>Done</code></td><td><code>{"},\"required\":[\"bytes\",\"shouted\",\"word\"]}"}</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A tag union is the one shape whose parser doesn't say what it holds: it asks the format for a tag's
				name, then reads that tag's payloads. So rocx gives the document <code>tags</code>, every name in the app's code (and
				its packages' and platforms') that could be a tag, and <code>Schema</code> offers each in turn. The
				parser turns down names not in the union; for the others it reads the payloads, and
				<code>Schema</code> writes their schemas. <code>{"[Red, Rgb(U8, U8, U8)]"}</code>
				is a string, <code>"Red"</code>, or an object, <code>{"{ \"Rgb\": [r, g, b] }"}</code>, as
				<code>Json</code> writes them.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A GET with events</strong> (<code>events!</code>) answers <code>text/event-stream</code>
					too: each event's data is JSON, with its schema, and an <code>end</code> event ends the stream.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A refusal</strong> is every method's default answer: its status, and why, as text.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A type of your own</strong> (<code>{"Email := Str.{ parser_for : _ }"}</code>) is described
					as <code>Json</code> reads it. Write its parser for any format (<code>encoding.parse_str</code>),
					not for JSON alone, and the document can describe it too.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>No static pages with params.</strong> This site's static copy writes the pages without
					params (<code>Paths.all</code>) and behind no directory's middleware but <code>routes/</code>'s. A
					page with params would need a list of the values to write.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One error page</strong>, the app's, at the root of <code>routes/</code>:
					<code>rocx build</code> refuses a <code>+error.rocx</code> anywhere else.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No rest params</strong> (<code>[...path]</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The OpenAPI document</strong> calls every JSON field required, describes a type that holds
					itself (a tree) only so many levels deep, and has no <code>$ref</code>s. Its title is the app's
					directory's name. A tag union's tags come from the names in the app's code, until Roc's parsers say
					which tags a union has, as they say a record's fields.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
